@startuml sequence_testing
title Testing Pipeline

participant pytest
participant "autouse\nfixture" as Fixture
participant globals
participant "@patch\ndecorators" as Mocks
participant TestMethod
participant SUT

== Test Collection ==

pytest -> pytest : collect test classes\nfrom tests/*.py

note over pytest
  10 test files covering:
  test_note, test_file, test_directory,
  test_app, test_config, test_data,
  test_anki_connect, test_format_converter,
  test_utils, test_debugger
end note

== Per-Test Execution ==

loop for each test method
  pytest -> Fixture : setup() [autouse=True, runs before every test]
  activate Fixture

  Fixture -> globals : reset relevant global state, e.g.:
  note right
    globals.CONFIG_DATA = {}
    globals.FIELDS_DICT = {"Basic": ["Front","Back"]}
    globals.NOTE_DICT_TEMPLATE = {...}
    globals.EXISTING_IDS = []
    globals.MEDIA = {}
    globals.FILE_HASHES = {}
    globals.ID_PREFIX = "ID: "
    (varies by test class)
  end note
  Fixture -> globals : recompile regexes\n(NOTE_REGEXP, INLINE_REGEXP, etc.)
  Fixture --> pytest : globals in known state
  deactivate Fixture

  pytest -> Mocks : apply @patch decorators (bottom-to-top)
  activate Mocks

  Mocks -> Mocks : mock AnkiConnect.invoke\n→ return_value = fake_note_ids
  Mocks -> Mocks : mock builtins.open\n→ mock_open(read_data="...")
  Mocks -> Mocks : mock os.rename, os.remove\n→ prevent filesystem writes
  Mocks -> Mocks : mock subprocess.Popen\n→ prevent Anki auto-launch
  note right
    Mock targets vary by test file:
    • test_anki_connect: urllib.request.urlopen
    • test_format_converter: format_converter.file_encode
    • test_utils: socket.create_connection, time.sleep
    • test_config: configparser.ConfigParser
    • test_data: json.load, json.dump
  end note
  Mocks --> pytest : patched environment ready
  deactivate Mocks

  pytest -> TestMethod : run test method(mock_arg1, mock_arg2, ...)
  activate TestMethod

  TestMethod -> SUT : create instance\n(Note, File, Directory, App, ...)
  activate SUT
  SUT -> globals : read CONFIG_DATA, FIELDS_DICT, etc.\n(pre-set by fixture to known values)
  SUT --> TestMethod : instance
  deactivate SUT

  TestMethod -> SUT : call method under test
  activate SUT
  SUT -> Mocks : call mocked dependency\n(AnkiConnect.invoke, open, os.rename, ...)
  Mocks --> SUT : return fake/controlled response
  SUT --> TestMethod : result
  deactivate SUT

  group Assertions
    TestMethod -> Mocks : mock.assert_called_once_with(expected_args)
    TestMethod -> Mocks : mock.assert_has_calls([call(...), call(...)])
    TestMethod -> TestMethod : assert result == expected_value
    TestMethod -> globals : assert globals.SOME_VAR == expected_state
  end

  TestMethod --> pytest : test passed / failed
  deactivate TestMethod

  pytest -> Mocks : teardown — restore all patched objects
end

== Global State Isolation Pattern ==

note over Fixture, globals
  Every test class has:
    @pytest.fixture(autouse=True)
    def setup(self):
        globals.CONFIG_DATA = {...}
        globals.FIELDS_DICT = {...}
        ...

  This ensures no state leaks between tests.
  Regex globals are recompiled from scratch
  because they depend on CONFIG_DATA values.
end note

== AnkiConnect Mock Layers ==

note over Mocks, SUT
  Three-layer mocking strategy:
  1. request(action, **params) — builds the request dict
     → mocked when testing request structure
  2. invoke(action, **params) — sends HTTP, returns result
     → most commonly mocked; skips real HTTP call
  3. parse(response) — validates and extracts result
     → tested directly in test_anki_connect.py
end note

@enduml
